Z||000033||lennyb4||06-23-2000||08:38 AM||||OK people..Heres where im at and what im setting up. Im going this weekend to get a PO box (mail boxes etc)that will be dedicated solely to this purpose. I would like to call it something like "Pennocks Fiero Meet" or something like that (ideas welcome).<BR>1.) First things first, we need to decide on a location. So far its Ohio,Kansas,Virgina, or Illinios. I would like to put it to a vote(majority rules)because i really cant start planning until we decide this. I will open a thread for this purpose.<BR>2.) Fierowife, thanks alot..as soon as we (the forum) decide on a location i'll contact you about some help.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>P.S.<BR> Just to let you all know, i do have some experiance in setting up large gatherings. So this isnt new to me but it is alot of work. But i feel the end result will be well worth it.||||reg||1
Z||000034||BBBAD GT||06-23-2000||09:38 AM||||Here is a suggestion, maybe Cliff can set up a new "forum" (like the tech and fiero chat) just for the purpose of this Pennock-Get-Together (PGT?) so not everyone would be hunting through the threads to see what progress has been made.  <P>I've mentioned it before, and I'll say it again, money has ALOT to do with this thing.  Not only setting up the place (all of the big names mentioned like Route 66 is EXPENSIVE with a capital E) but just getting to the place and hotel/food.  Just keep that in the back of your minds please, I'd hate to see a good idea crash and burn just as quickly as it started.  <P>    ||||reg||1
Z||000035||Monkeyman||06-23-2000||10:58 AM||||I vote for Mid-Ohio.  I live in Indiana so I could do it.  If it was much farther away, I don't think I could afford the time off of work.  That's something to consider, too.  Some may not have the time to drive even half way across the country to the PFM (Pennocks Fiero Meet).  What do we do about them??  I don't really care about making history or being in the Guinness Book of World Records, I just want to meet the rest of my PFF brothers and sisters.||||reg||1
Z||000036||Gold-86SE||06-23-2000||11:11 AM||||<BR>Not to sound like I am complaining, but what about us Fiero Freaks in SOUTH TEXAS, FLORIDA, or CALIFORNIA?<P>Not to bash Canadian Fiero Owners, but making it closer for Canadians is not helpful to the southern U.S. Fiero Owners. And I would venture to say that there are more Fiero onwers in the southern states than all of Canada.<P>If I had to make that drive, it would take me 2 to 3 days.  I'd have to drive half way across the freakin' continent, forget it. If I have to drive that far for just an event I'd rather stay home.<P>I get the impression, that the North East Fiero owners like to have things close to them.  What is wrong with locating an event in the middle of the United States, omewhere around, preferably, Oklahoma or (second best) Kansas?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000037||ChadMan||06-23-2000||11:15 AM||||It would be really cool if we could rent Mid-Ohio, but I share Old Lar's concerns about the expense.  Mid-Ohio is a first-class facility and consequently, it's really pricey.  I'm not sure what it costs to rent for a day w/EMT's but I bet it's at least $3500.  Their schedule is surprisingly full, too.<P>How about Nelson Ledges, near Cleveland?  It's not as nice, but it's much cheaper.<P>All I know is, if I can get my Fiero on a real road course for a full day for less than $100, and that road course is no more than 8 hours from Columbus, OH, I'm in.||||reg||1
Z||000038||Oreif||06-23-2000||11:29 AM||||I just took an Atlas and drew some circles and found that somewhere around Topeka Kansas is centrally located between California, Florida, and Maine. Maybe this would be the best for all.||||reg||3
Z||000039||Ozzy||06-23-2000||12:41 PM||||I agree with Orief guys. It needs to be in da middle of the country somewhere. I would legitamately consider driving to the mid west from Utah but not the East coast. <P>Does it have to be a track based type event at a real race track? There are all kinds of alternatives that would be much more economical if you flex the criteria a little.<P>I would think just everyone getting together on a grand scale would be a blast. If it is in the country somewhere then there would be all kinds of country roads to go blasting down for "Test Rides".<P>Think about Woodstock!! It was on a farm. Forget the fact that there were riots and dead people. The concept was good. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Just a thought<P>Ozzy<P>P.S. Don't mean to sound redundant here folks. Ya'wl mentioned this stuff above, I know. Lenny...so far this is your gig. If you ask the forum for opinions you will get 1000+ of them...all different! I suggest you put it together with some select help, have it where ever you want, and take your chances. If us western folks cant make it...tough tittys I guess! Maybe we have our own...yeah... like AFL and NFL!!! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> (Joking)<p>[This message has been edited by Ozzy (edited 06-23-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000040||William Federle||06-23-2000||01:13 PM||||Topeka or Ohio would work for me. They're both within a days drive or so. A three day weekend would allow one day there with a day on either side to travel. If we had enough people going, $1500.00 or so wouldn't be that bad for a facility - $3.00 ea for 500 people. It might be a good idea to plan on it for a year or so from now to allow people to maybe plan a day or two of vacation for it.<p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 06-23-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000041||Old Lar||06-23-2000||01:46 PM||||You can already see the concern's arriving.  Time off, location, $$, up front money, what if it rains, last minute registers.  Plan for 100 and 200 show up: not enough "goodie" bags, T shirts etc. and the complaints or worse plan for 100 and 25 show up =lose money, who takes that in the shorts?<P>Some where in the mid west is the central location.  For me Kansas is a two day drive, one way.  How far are you willing to drive for a one day show?  Does that mean you need a multiple day show.  By all means, with stuff to do in the area.  I can't (or wouldn't) race my Fiero for several days.  You don't drive to the track the car you plan to run, you trailer it so you can get home if something breaks.<P>The Florida weekend is piggy backed onto the Daytona Spring Spectacular, with thousands of cars, nice weather in the spring for us yankees.  Lots to do in Florida and you are only an hour drive to Disney/Universal. For me this year it was 2 days down, 3 days at Daytona, 2 days back.  A 7 day commitment.  In the past I've spent 3 days at Disney/Universal a 10 day commitment.<P>The Wisconsin Dells show has lots to do in the area from casino gambling to multiple theme parks and its held at a nice resort with lots to do there. A 7 day commitment.  Two days out, 3-4 days of show and 2 days home.<P>FOCOA shows try to get into areas with "stuff" to do. This years show  in Louisville has tours of the breweries, steam boat rides on the river etc.  Expensive, by all means.  I can make Louisville in one long days drive.  But its for a 2-3 day stay, then a long days drive home. so I'm talking of a minimum of a 5 day road trip.<P>FOCONE show in Saco this weekend is a nice place.  Maine is nice this time of year.  There is lots to do in that area, Old Orchard Beach, whale watching on the ocean.  Maine is a nice tourist area.  I'll miss it this year, because I just found out the time (my fault).  It would be a days drive one way for me.  Plus I have frinds in Kennebunkport I can stay with.  George and Barbara don't mind at all.<P>It is a real challenge to put on a show/gathering or anything of the sorts.  Until you've been in the planning and successful execution you'll see if 51% of the people are happy, you've done good.<P>||||reg||11
